  "solution": {
    "js": "function twoLoneValues(values) {\n  let both = 0\n  for (const value of values) both ^= value\n  const bit = both & -both\n  let first = 0\n  for (const value of values) if (value & bit) first ^= value\n  const second = both ^ first\n  return first < second ? [first, second] : [second, first]\n}\n",
    "py": "def two_lone_values(values):\n    both = 0\n    for value in values:\n        both ^= value\n    bit = both & -both\n    first = 0\n    for value in values:\n        if value & bit:\n            first ^= value\n    second = both ^ first\n    return sorted([first, second])\n",
    "explanation": "XOR-ing every value leaves a ^ b, which is non-zero because a and b differ. Its lowest set bit separates a from b, and both copies of every paired value land in the same group, so XOR-ing one group cancels its pairs and leaves exactly one lone value. The other lone value is (a ^ b) ^ that value."
  },
